A||N||2||pherder||Weekend from HECK!   ... long.||||8||
Z||000000||pherder||05-14-2000||06:49 PM||||This was the weekend I was going to fire up the HO Quad 4 Coupe ... heavy on the "WAS"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Put oil in the engine, finished up the "little" details and was about to put in the anti-freeze/water and thought ... I think I will do an Archie (Of V8 Archie) and try to fire this puppy BEFORE I go an make it all wet.  So I put in the battery, hooked up the positive, tried to hook up the negative ... hmmmm, no end on the wire that hooks to the battery tray.  Looked around the shop and found another negative cable that fit the Quad 4 better and installed that.  Thoses of you who have taken on and off the negative battery cable know that it sits back under the frame.  And of course, it was twisted.  Several blue words later ... it is connected!  I have power to the car.  Start around to the driver's door, open it and ... no lights.  Hmmm?  Go back to the battery, get out a VOM and start checking ... power at the battery, power at the cable down at the power block, hmmm no power on the small stud.  Look closer and find that the strip of metal that the Fiero uses to bridge the two studs didn't make it to this wiring harness.  Unhook the negative cable from the battery.  Start removing the nuts off the studs and find I don't have the proper nut driver for the small stud.  Found something that works, sort of and remove the nut.  Look around the shop for a piece of metal to bridge the gap, find one that has holes in it that just might work ... NOT!  Get drill out and make it fit.  Start attaching the terminals to the power block and then proceed to attach the negative battery cable to the battery.  Did I mention what a pain it is to make the cable twist AND start screwing in the side post while putting your arms and hands in positions they were never intented to be in?  Get the negative battery cable attached and test the connections.  Hmmmmm?  NO power what so ever!  What is going on?  Hmmmm?   Maybe I should attach ALL the cables first to the power block ... like the one from the POSITIVE battery cable?  There is sits, down by all the grounded metal ... swiftly I grabbed it and pulled it away from the nasty grounded metal and while holding it, disconnect the negative battery cable ... Did I mention ...?  I am sure I did.  Anyway, I re-attached the POSITIVE power lead to the power block and then re-attached the negative battery cable to the battery.   Did I mention ...?  ARRGGGG!!!!!<P>Wait!!!!  Do I hear relays clicking?  Power stuff arching (as I try to get the $#@! negative battery cable to tighted up)?  YES!!!  I now have a power connection.<P>Walk to the driver's side, open the door and see on the instrutment panel the Check Engine light, the Door Ajar light, the Battery light ... all that you would expect when turning the key on.  One problem ... the keys were on top of the car on the sun roof!  The ignition was in the off position.<P>I turned around, went back to the battery and removed the negative battery cable.<P>This was yesterday afternoon and I haven't worked on that car since.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Looks like I will have to drop the engine assembly and remove the wiring harness, disassemble the wiring harness and find out what is wrong, fix it, and reassemble it, put the engine assembly back in the car and hook back up everything.  Glad I didn't put antifreeze in it.<P>AAAARRRRRGGGGGGHHHHHHHHHHH!!!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||05-14-2000||08:10 PM||||Hehehehe... I feel for you man, really, I do.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||7
Z||000002||batboy||05-14-2000||10:33 PM||||So, what's the problem, Paul? That just sounds like a typical day of working on my Batmobile. Been there and done that, well sort of anyway.||||reg||1
